WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.080
Ralph Breaks the Internet is like an Easter
egg hunt on a lawn made out of Easter eggs,

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:08.940
with references to pop culture of the past
baked into the movie's very DNA.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.700
If you've already seen the Wreck-It Ralph
sequel at least once, here are a few callbacks

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.619
and inside jokes you might have missed.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:16.600
Ralph Breaks the Internet doesn't spend much

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.130
time on human characters.

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:22.570
Beyond a few anonymous gamers and internet
users, this movie is solely focused on its

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:26.880
digital creations — creatures of the arcade
cabinet or the World Wide Web.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.000
Any other approach would take precious screen
time away from the denizens of Tapper.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.960
There are still a few moments where the movie
checks in with people on the other side of

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:35.460
the monitor.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.580
If you noticed any conspicuous cutaways to
carbon-based lifeforms, you were probably

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:44.150
noticing the some of the movie's cast of real
YouTubers and internet personalities.

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:48.960
Among the stars making an appearance are comedian
Colleen Ballinger (known for her character

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.630
Miranda Sings), actress Dani Fernandez, comedian
Flula Borg, and streamer Tiffany Herrera,

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:55.780
better known as Cupquake.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.580
Ralph Breaks the Internet also includes another
nod to online video culture with the YouTube

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:05.330
Play Button visible in the office of Yes,
the personified algorithm who runs the show

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:05.900
at Buzztube.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:10.640
If you aren't familiar, YouTube Play Buttons
are real-life physical plaques that the company

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.540
gifts out to content creators for passing
milestones in their subscriber count.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:19.000
There's a silver button for channels that
exceed 100,000 subscribers, a gold button

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.380
for those that pass a million, and a diamond
button for ones that go past 10 million.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:27.380
The one in the background of the Ralph sequel
looks like a gold one, indicating that the

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:31.320
fictional content company still has a ways
to go before it gets to the top of the heap.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:36.400
It's no surprise that there's an entire galaxy
of references to the Star Wars universe in

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:40.270
Ralph Breaks the Internet — particularly
once Ralph and Vanellope make their way to

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.120
Disney property.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.720
There are obvious drop-ins like stormtrooper
security guards and a cameo from C-3PO, but

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.420
there are also smaller touches that hearken
back to the galaxy far, far away.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:55.860
A couple of examples include an R2-D2-themed
tie on one of the office workers, killing

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.960
time by sharing Ralph's viral videos.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:02.460
You can also see some collectible-looking
Star Wars-themed plates on display in Mr.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:03.730
Litwak's office.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.920
Fortnite
Ralph goes through a few options while trying

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.260
to come up with content for his viral video
scheme.

00:02:08.649 --> 00:02:10.179
What trends should he be chasing?

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:14.790
Well, there are screaming goat videos, which
are back in style, as well as bee pun videos

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.320
that never go out of style.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:20.680
But there's also a clip from the mega-popular
video game Fortnite, briefly focusing on the

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:24.320
floating battle bus full of players that kicks
off every Battle Royale.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.180
If that wasn't enough, the movie also shows
Ralph indulging in a bona fide Fortnite dance

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:30.740
— specifically, the floss.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:34.200
We hope you thanked the bus driver for this
experience on your way home from the theater.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.819
While Wreck-It Ralph presented a Toy Story-esque
look at what happens at the arcade when no

00:02:38.819 --> 00:02:42.930
one's looking, Ralph Breaks the Internet takes
its heroes to the sprawling metropolis of

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.220
the World Wide Web.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:49.320
In that sense, the sequel has less in common
with Toy Story and more in common with Zootopia,

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:53.520
the 2016 Disney animated feature about a vibrant
city in the animal kingdom.

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:58.700
The most obvious Easter egg is an appearance
from shady protagonist Nick Wilde, who appears

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.550
near Disney's section of the internet.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:05.280
But a smaller reference to Zootopia can be
found in the online game Slaughter Race, as

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.560
evidenced by a sign for the Mr. Big Limo Service.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:12.640
The fact that the reference is in Slaughter
Race raises some head-scratching questions,

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:13.140
though.

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:17.240
Has Zootopia turned into an apocalyptic urban
battlefield?

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.239
Or does Slaughter Race just have a gritty
Zootopia level?

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:24.120
Either way, it's no version of the city we'd
want to see Judy Hopps have to deal with.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.540
During Ralph and Vanellope's first visit to

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:31.530
eBay, the two are presented with rows of different
kiosks, each auctioning off some peculiar

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:32.020
item.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.140
Most of the stuff isn't worth much, but at
least one item in the online auction house

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:37.760
is treasure straight out of the Disney vault.

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:43.000
In the background, viewers can see the magic,
genie-summoning lamp from Aladdin up for bidding.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.480
We're not actually sure if the genie himself
would be included.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:50.440
Interestingly, Aladdin's Jasmine can later
be seen wielding her own magic lamp as a weapon

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:53.050
when Vanellope intrudes on the princess lounge.

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:54.540
Maybe they're more common than we thought?

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.120
After Ralph and Vanellope arrive at the "Oh

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.700
My Disney" corner of the internet, they literally
bump into a dearly-departed icon of comic

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:03.180
books and pop culture.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.760
While the final version of the cameo is just
a quick reveal of Stan Lee turning around

00:04:07.769 --> 00:04:12.290
to look toward camera after Vanellope knocks
into him, the surprising appearance wasn't

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:14.180
initially meant to be so fleeting.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:17.920
Originally, Lee's cameo was going to be a
more involved sequence.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:21.689
Co-director Phil Johnston explained the details
to The Wrap, saying,

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:26.120
"We had this thing called 'Marvelize Yourself,'
where the human avatar is able to go through

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:29.159
what looks like a metal detector and turn
into a Marvel character.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:32.759
And as part of that scene, we had Stan Lee
go through the Marvelizer.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:37.460
But when we decided to lose that joke [...] we
still wanted to have Stan Lee in the movie,

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:38.780
because we love Stan Lee."

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.900
In case you missed it, Ralph Breaks the Internet
also features two post-credits sequences.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:48.419
One, an extended riff on the Disney princesses
scene, makes its appearance halfway through

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:49.140
the credits.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:53.619
But another, more involved sequence doesn't
kick in until the very end of the movie — and

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.100
it's an emotional roller coaster, to say the
least.

00:04:56.380 --> 00:05:00.819
The final moments of the movie are disguised
as a sneak peek for Disney's hotly-anticipated

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:01.800
Frozen sequel.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:06.360
Just as the sneak peek seems to be getting
off the ground, the first look is abruptly

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:11.279
cut off by Ralph in the vein of a classic
Rick Roll — a vintage internet gag centering

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.310
on Rick Astley's "Never Gonna GIve You Up.".

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.499
It's a move that shows some real moxie, teasing
younger Frozen fans with an ice-cold joke

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.440
that only their parents are probably going
to get.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.880
It just goes to show you, the internet can
be kind of a cruel place.

